    <div class="page">
        <div class="section-header">DISMANTLING AND REASSEMBLY</div>

        <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 20px;">
            <div style="width: 48%;">
                <p><strong>WARNING</strong> - Process fluids and/or pressure retained in the transmitter primary can unite severe injury and death or damage to the equipment. It is necessary to be able to safely isolate the transmitter from the process before removing the instrument from service or when draining or venting.</p>
                <p><strong>CAUTION</strong> - Dismantling and reassembly should not be carried out on site because of the risk of environmental conditions such as humidity, dust, etc. The dismantling and reassembly procedure given below should be carried out in the listed order to avoid instrument damage.</p>
                <p><strong>Required tools</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>2 mm Allen key</li>
                    <li>3 mm Allen key</li>
                    <li>Small Phillips screwdriver</li>
                    <li>17 mm spanner</li>
                    <li>17 mm torque wrench</li>
                    <li>(Range = 52 Nm - 70 lbs ft)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div style="width: 48%;">
                <p><strong>Reassembly</strong></p>
                <p>Check that the "O" rings are not damaged; if in replace.</p>
                <p><strong>WARNING</strong> - Assembling flanges with incorrect fixing bolts and nuts and improper "O" rings can cause fracture or overstressing of bolts and release of pressurized process fluid. Only use specified torque values. Follow the procedure described herebelow described and do not exceed the specified torque limits. DO NOT REMOVE the "O" ring fitted in the sensor neck; it provides the housing a degree of protection.</p>
                <p>a) Replace flange bolts with a torque of 20 Nm (15 ft lbs) using a 17 mm torque wrench.</p>
                <p>b) Insert the sensor cable in its recess at the bottom of the transmitter.</p>
                <p>c) Screw the housing down completely until the nesting of housing/sensor assembly is reached, then unscrew by one complete turn maximum. Rotate the topwork in the desired position and lock it with the tang grub screw.</p>
                <p>d) Plug the sensor cable to the secondary electronics. Fix the electronic circuit by its screws.</p>
                <p>e) Refit the covers and tighten securely.</p>
                <p><strong>WARNING</strong> - For Hazardous Location installations, at least eight (8) threads on the cover must be engaged in order to meet the flameproof (explosion-proof) requirements.</p>
                <p>f) Unscrew the cover locking screw to secure the covers. This is mandatory to meet "flameproof requirements" for Hazardous Areas Installation.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
            <p><strong>PRESSURE TEST WARRING</strong></p>
            <p>Before assembling the flanges and the transducer, a pressure test is required. At this purpose, apply a hydrostatic pressure of the maximum overpressure allowed by the transmitter to both process connections simultaneously. Wait for one minute, then verify that no leakages occurred, otherwise repeat the assembly procedure and the pressure test.</p>
        </div>

